  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Gbemisola Adeoti is a Nigerian poet and critic who teaches at Obafemi Awolowo University, Ile-Ife.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Naked Soles is Adeoti's collection; the title image — bare feet on hard ground — is of exposure rather than protection.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The shrinking stanza is part of the structure: the net closes as the poem proceeds.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Eight, six, three and four lines give twenty-one.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    There is no fixed metre and no rhyme scheme; the poem is held together by its sequence of metaphors and by its run-on lines.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The whale takes the water, the tiger the land, the hawk the air.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It takes the whole apparatus, which is why the loss is of the attempt and not only of the catch.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    "Aborting" is a birth word applied to a hope: the dreams are ended before they can be delivered.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The stanza gives one cause: the whale has taken the gear and the catch, so there is nothing to stay for.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It is the international maritime signal for departure, and the poem flies it over "empty ships" — a bitter contradiction.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Peter the fisherman disciple, the name Peter, and the verb "to peter out" are collapsed into one sound, so failure is built into the language.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    "To peter out" means to diminish gradually and end weakly, which is worse than a single catastrophe.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The tiger "cries deep in the glade" and takes the land and the forest.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The creature is prehistoric, which makes the violence feel older than the state and therefore harder to resist.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    "While infants shudder home" — fear reaches the youngest, and home is where they flee to.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    "Gut" here is courage and entrail at once; snatching it back is a gesture of panic and shame.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Tribulation is given the bayonet, so that suffering itself becomes an armed force.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    "Halting" and "venturous" pull against each other: the people go on, but fearfully, and at the worst hour.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The giant hawk "courts unceasing disaster" as it "hovers and hoots in space".
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Courtship implies pursuit; the predator does not wait for the disaster, it invites it.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Three elements, three predators, no fourth place to escape to — the sequence is a siege.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The turn to direct address is the poem's warning, and it is the only place a future appears.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    "Patiently" turns the landscape into an agent that can afford to wait, which is what makes it an ambusher.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The ambush is aimed at the people who try to leave — the trap is sprung on hope itself.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The direction of nothing is the life the poem has been describing; the people point away from it.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It is desirable and guarded: the water belongs to the whale, so the shore is a direction rather than a destination.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The poem gives a direction and no assurance; that balance is what makes the ending moving rather than sentimental.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A quality of persons is given to the land, which is how the landscape becomes a character with intentions.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Present-tense verbs — swallows, cries, hovers, lies — make the poem a report on a condition that is happening now.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The poem speaks in the third person and then in direct address, but never says "I"; the observer stays outside.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Sentences run across line ends and stanza breaks, which drives the reader forward the way the people are driven.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The shortening stanzas are a visual analogue of a net tightening around the people.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A poem naming a regime dates; a poem naming a food chain does not, and the indirection is also a form of protection.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The term is precise, maritime and ironic in its context, and the irony is the point of the question.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The argument is cumulative: each stanza closes one element, so that stanza four can name the trap.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Fear reaches the youngest and the oldest in consecutive lines, which is how the poem shows that no age is exempt.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The shore is named as a direction and the land is named as the ambush; the poem ends by holding both.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The shared subject is blocked hope; the difference is that Vanity blames the people's deafness and Ambush blames the land.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The concealment and the waiting are the point, and they explain why the land is said to lie "patiently" ahead.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The argument holds the constraint and the direction together, which is exactly what the last stanza does.
  ]

]

